1. You would have to count to **one thousand** before you use the letter "A" in the English spelling of a whole number.  
   (One, two, three... up to nine hundred ninety-nine contain no "A"; "one thousand" is the first.)

2. "The quick brown fox **jumps over the lazy dog**."  
   This sentence is famous for containing every letter of the English alphabet at least once (a pangram).

3. **The Three Little Pigs and Little Red Riding Hood**  

Once upon a time, three little pigs set out to build their homes. The first built one of straw, the second of sticks, and the third of bricks. Nearby, Little Red Riding Hood was walking through the woods to her grandmother’s house, carrying a basket of treats.  

The Big Bad Wolf, hungry and clever, first blew down the straw house. The first pig escaped to the stick house. The wolf blew that down too, and both pigs ran to the brick house.  

Meanwhile, the wolf spotted Little Red Riding Hood. He tricked her into picking flowers while he ran to Grandma’s house, disguised himself, and waited. But the third pig, wise and brave, saw the wolf’s plan from his brick house window. He shouted a warning to Little Red Riding Hood.  

She remembered her grandmother’s lessons and ran straight to the brick house. The wolf chased her, but the pigs locked the door just in time. The wolf huffed and puffed, but the brick house stood strong.  

Defeated, the wolf slunk away. Inside, the three pigs and Little Red Riding Hood shared the basket of treats with Grandma, who had arrived safely through the back door. And they all lived happily ever after. [end of text]


